nodecode-afterforge is a rewrite of ussyverse's ussyverse/afterforge:

  https://github.com/ussyverse/afterforge
  commit 067a739e5ab64097140c5200bde2cc5d52662ea5
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Agent Fix Lab contributors (LICENSE)

The rewrite is the plugin's loop, in Common Lisp against the Nodecode cell
contract: a failure or a correction recorded as a candidate case, a person's
approval, a drafted check, and a run of that check whose output is kept as
evidence. What it keeps: the shape of the eight tools as six (a case
recorded, the shelf listed, a decision taken, a check drafted, the check run,
the evidence reported), the digest a check is approved by, the status a case
moves through -- candidate, reviewed, rejected, verified, regressed -- and the
rule that a check runs only after it has been approved and that its history
is bounded. The pytest recipe of the original is generalised to any shell
command run through `sh`, the project folder it runs in, and the exit code
that means the check passed.

What is left out, and why: the scan of Hermes' own state.db for corrections
and the post_tool_call / on_session_end capture hooks, because they are
Hermes' history and its hook points, and a Nodecode cell has its own; the
local web page and the `uv`-managed Python runtime with its three git
dependencies, because the shelf is now a folder of JSON files and the check
runs with the shell the user already has; the pytest-specific recipe builder,
because the command is now given rather than generated; and the pre_verify
reminder the plugin could add to a turn, because this cell does not hook the
turn.

Nothing of the original's Python is bundled. The cell's own code is Common
Lisp, and it runs no program except the checks the operator approved.
